{"title":"Breaking one factor into two smaller factors","key_idea":"When one factor can be split into two smaller factors, both sides of the multiplication stay equal if you keep all the numbers multiplied together.","method":["Look at the factor you need to split (the second number on the left side).","Find two smaller numbers that multiply to make that factor.","Write those two numbers in place of the original factor on the right side.","Check by multiplying: the left side and right side should give the same answer."],"worked_example":{"problem":"7 x 15 = 7 x 5 x __","steps":["The factor 15 must be split into two smaller numbers.","Find two numbers that multiply to make 15: 5 and 3.","Write 3 in the blank: 7 x 15 = 7 x 5 x 3.","Check: 7 x 15 = 105 and 7 x 5 x 3 = 35 x 3 = 105. Both sides match."],"answer":"3"},"key_terms":[{"term":"factor","meaning":"a number being multiplied"}],"common_mistakes":["Forgetting to check that the two new factors multiply to make the original factor.","Writing numbers that do not multiply to the original factor."],"remember":"The two smaller factors must always multiply together to equal the original factor.","quick_check":[{"q":"9 x 10 = 9 x 2 x __; what number goes in the blank?","a":"5, because 2 x 5 = 10"},{"q":"5 x 16 = 5 x 4 x __; what number goes in the blank?","a":"4, because 4 x 4 = 16"}],"tell_your_child":"When you split a number into two parts, make sure those two parts multiply back to the number you started with. Try this: can you write 12 as two numbers multiplied together in different ways?"}
